<p>This is a quick post about the ‘post appreciation buttons’, in case you are a regular visitor, and were wondering why they looked different suddenly.</p><p>I used to use <a href="https://iine.to">iine</a> to power the little heart buttons that sat in each post heading. I’m not sure if there’s a catch-all term for this kind of thing, but they are emoji buttons visitors can click to show some kind of support/appreciation/mild approbation for the writing in that post. You click the thing, it adds 1 to a counter and displays that next to the emoji, and I get a sense that at least some real humans read the page, not just AI scrapers.</p><p>I used iine because it was privacy-protecting, and it was possible to self-host it. I followed their instructions to set it up at <a href="https://supabase.com">Supabase</a>, but while this worked fine, Supabase is a massively powerful sledgehammer to crack a very tiny and soft-shelled nut. I didn’t really want to maintain <em>another</em> service just for this little, nice-to-have thing.</p><p>Luckily for me, Adam Newbold, aka <a href="https://www.neatnik.net">Neatnik</a> — Maker of All The Things — has just released <a href="https://neatstats.net">Neatstats</a>: a web stats service which has ‘updoots’ (a post appreciation button) integrated into it. I have been using the stats service for a while in beta (it’s great!), but now I can also use the updoots. It’s still a heart (but looks a bit different), but all the counters are set back to zero until I find out if it’s possible to populate them with the exported data from Supabase.</p><p>That’s it really — click one if you like ❤.️</p>